# alloc_vecpool_2026-09-10 — allocator-call reduction, port vs C

commit:  (this change)
host:    lilith (WSL2, Ryzen 9 7950X, 23 GiB), x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
tool:    heaptrack + heaptrack_print, via tools/heaptrack_alloc_cell.sh
cell:    identity_run crop:codec-corpus/gb82-sc/windows.png 512 320 qp20 preset -1
         (screen-content still; the whole MD funnel runs, and it is small enough
         to heaptrack in seconds)
C arm:   tools/capture_c_trace/capture_c_trace.bin 512 320 20 -1 p.yuv c.obu

## WHY this cell and this metric

The user's instruction was "heaptrack vs C and fix our memory model deficits
that are amplifying allocs". The deficit is NOT peak heap — the port's peak is
a THIRD of C's. It is allocator CALL COUNT, where the port was 14.7x C:

    port  6,851,798 calls    peak heap 25.44 M
    C       466,395 calls    peak heap 69.43 M

C is low because it allocates its mode-decision working set ONCE per encoder
thread in `svt_aom_mode_decision_context_ctor` (md_process.c:214) and reuses it
for every tx-type trial of every transform unit of every block of every frame.
The port allocated the same buffers per call.

## EVERY ARM IS BYTE-IDENTICAL

Every number below was taken with the emitted bitstream hashed. All arms, and
the C reference, produce the SAME 14,871-byte OBU:

    sha256(first 16) = 00c327d57ee3fc1d

An arm whose allocation count moved but whose bytes moved too is not an
allocation result, so the hash is printed by the harness on every run.

## RESULTS

    arm                                            calls        vs base   peak heap
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    base (6a784743)                            6,851,798             --     25.44 M
    smallvec on txb_eob/cul/type + MDS3 locals 6,353,616         -7.3 %     25.44 M
    + PoolVec on the tx pipeline's outputs     3,420,176        -50.1 %     27.78 M
    + PoolVec on the MD scratch buffers        2,504,946        -63.4 %     27.69 M
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    C reference                                  466,395                    69.43 M

Peak heap rose 2.25 M (+8.8 %) and stays at 40 % of C's. That is the free
lists holding their buffers: POOL_DEPTH (64) per element type per thread.

## WHAT WAS TRIED AND REJECTED

`SmallVec<[i32; 16]>` for `Cand::txb_q` (the per-TXB coefficient rows).
MEASURED WORSE: 6,353,616 -> 6,492,430. Inline 16 covers only a 4x4 TXB; the
8x8-and-larger majority spill to the heap AND pay a copy, because the value
stored there is MOVED out of the tx scratch today rather than copied. Reverted;
the field is pooled instead, which keeps the move. The comment on the field
records this so it is not re-attempted.

## SITES, IN THE ORDER heaptrack RANKED THEM

Taken with CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=line-tables-only into a separate
target dir, so the frames carry file:line through the inlining.

    1,661,174  tx_pipeline.rs:873  grown_out(&mut out.qcoeff, pw*ph)
                 spread over txt_search, chroma::eval_uv, inject_candidates,
                 mds3, md_cfl_rd_pick_alpha and run_mds1 -- no single caller
                 dominated, which is why the fix is at the Vec level
      660,053  inject.rs:163       u_pred/v_pred/u_pred10/v_pred10
      548,918  chroma.rs:80        eval_uv's u_pred/v_pred
      452,676  mds3.rs:707         dep_recon / dep_pred / dep_pred10
       72,392  mds3.rs:1128        d0_recon = dep_recon.clone()

## RUNTIME COST — the budget was 1 %, MEASURED at 0.43-1.23 %

Instruction counts, `perf stat -e instructions`, PINNED to one P-core
(`taskset -c 2`) because this host (i265) is a hybrid P/E-core Core Ultra 7
265K and unpinned wall clock moves several percent with core placement alone.
Baseline binary built from 6a784743 in a sibling extraction; both are
`examples/perf_encode gradient <n> <n> 40 <preset>`, 3 frames.

    cell                base            candidate       delta
    ---------------------------------------------------------
    256x256 preset 2    14,529,697,574  14,592,064,246  +0.43 %
    512x512 preset 6     2,411,255,071   2,428,499,075  +0.72 %
    1024x1024 preset 8   2,534,652,799   2,554,722,601  +0.79 %
    256x256 preset 6       704,868,621     710,891,895  +0.85 %
    512x512 preset 10      482,579,555     488,528,562  +1.23 %

The cost rises as the preset gets FASTER, which is the expected shape: at
preset 10 there is less mode-decision work per pooled buffer, so the free-list
bookkeeping is a larger share. `_int_malloc` drops out of the profile entirely
(2.45 % of preset-6 self time in the baseline, below the 1 % cut in the
candidate).

Wall clock agrees but is much noisier on this host. Interleaved paired rounds
(`tools/perf_ab.sh`, 11 rounds, order randomised per round), all cells
byte-identical, worst cell 256x256 preset 6 at ratio 1.0156. The SAME-BINARY
null arm on that cell is ratio 0.9996 with p25/p75 0.9970/1.0017, so the wall
clock difference is real and not drift -- it is just a worse estimator than the
instruction count above.

## TWO MICRO-DECISIONS, BOTH MEASURED

* `Cell<Vec<Vec<T>>>` (take / pop / set) INSTEAD of `RefCell`: SLOWER.
  489,282,103 vs 488,528,562 instructions on 512x512 preset 10. The borrow flag
  is cheaper than moving the list header twice. The `RefCell` stays.
* `grown_out` takes from the free list LAZILY rather than `TxOutBufs::default`
  taking both buffers eagerly: 1.02 % -> 0.83 % on 256x256 preset 6. Most calls
  pass `need_recon == false` and never touch `recon`, so an eager take paid a
  thread-local round trip per call for a buffer nobody used.
* Bypassing the pool for the ZEROED scratch sites (`vec![0; n]` again) was
  measured too, in case their `resize` memset was the cost: 489,304,613 vs
  488,528,562 on 512x512 preset 10, i.e. pooling them is slightly BETTER. The
  residual cost is the tx-pipeline bookkeeping, not the zero fill.

